17:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1f",null,{"guide":{"date":"June 25–26, 1862","place":"Oak Grove along the Williamsburg Road south of the Chickahominy River, followed by Mechanicsville and Beaver Dam Creek north of the river, near Richmond, Virginia","result":"Oak Grove ended inconclusively after George B. McClellan gained about 600 yards without disrupting Robert E. Lee’s planned offensive. At Beaver Dam Creek the next day, Fitz John Porter and George A. McCall repulsed direct Confederate attacks from a prepared position. Lee’s army suffered substantially heavier losses, but McClellan ordered Porter to withdraw during the night, surrendered the battlefield, and yielded the operational initiative to Lee","facts":[{"label":"Episode scope","value":"The first two engagements of the Seven Days Battles","note":"The episode covers Oak Grove on June 25 and Beaver Dam Creek, also called Mechanicsville or Ellerson’s Mill, on June 26."},{"label":"Oak Grove objective","value":"Advance the United States line toward high ground near Old Tavern","note":"McClellan wanted ground suitable for siege guns that could threaten Richmond. The episode places the intended advance at about one and one-half miles."},{"label":"Oak Grove opening","value":"About 8:30 a.m. on June 25","note":"Joseph Hooker sent Daniel Sickles’s brigade on the right and Cuvier Grover’s brigade in the center; Philip Kearny sent John C. Robinson’s brigade on the left."},{"label":"Terrain at Oak Grove","value":"Wooded and swampy ground disrupted the advancing line","note":"Sickles’s brigade fell behind in the difficult terrain, leaving Grover unsupported on his right when Grover met Ambrose R. Wright’s Confederates."},{"label":"Remote direction","value":"McClellan first directed the action by telegraph from about three miles away","note":"He ordered a withdrawal after receiving reports of heavy fighting, reached the front at about 1 p.m., assessed the field, and ordered the lost ground retaken."},{"label":"Oak Grove result","value":"Indecisive; the United States line advanced about 600 yards","note":"The limited gain did not interrupt the Confederate offensive scheduled for June 26."},{"label":"Oak Grove casualties","value":"United States 626; Confederacy 441","note":"The National Park Service gives 1,067 total casualties. The standard breakdown is United States: 68 killed, 503 wounded, 55 missing; Confederacy: 66 killed, 362 wounded, 13 missing. The automated transcript drops a digit and renders the Confederate wounded as “36.”"},{"label":"Lee’s risk","value":"Most Confederate striking power shifted north of the Chickahominy","note":"The episode states that about 25,000 Confederates remained south of the river to defend Richmond. Strength figures vary by source and method of counting."},{"label":"McClellan’s intelligence","value":"Reports indicated that Jackson was moving toward the exposed Federal right","note":"McClellan correctly suspected the direction of Lee’s intended blow but did not reinforce the right before Lee attacked."},{"label":"Planned Confederate trigger","value":"Jackson turn the Federal right early on June 26","note":"Lee expected Jackson’s movement to set Branch, A. P. Hill, Longstreet, and D. H. Hill in motion in sequence."},{"label":"Jackson’s delayed march","value":"Slash Church at about 9 a.m., roughly six hours behind the planned schedule","note":"The episode attributes delay to a late start, obstacles cut by United States cavalry, unfamiliar roads, and cautious movement. Exact expectations and responsibility are matters of historical interpretation."},{"label":"A. P. Hill’s decision","value":"Crossed Meadow Bridge at about 3 p.m. without confirmation from Jackson","note":"Hill acted without first notifying Lee or obtaining new permission. The episode also notes evidence that Lee had independently concluded that a crossing should occur before dark."},{"label":"Porter’s defensive choice","value":"Withdrew outposts through Mechanicsville to Beaver Dam Creek","note":"Porter intended to fight from the prepared east-bank position rather than hold the village."},{"label":"Beaver Dam Creek position","value":"Steep, swampy approach, felled-tree obstacles, earthworks, and supporting artillery","note":"The National Park Service describes a strong position with open fields of fire. Its vulnerable point was the northern flank that Jackson was supposed to turn."},{"label":"Forces at Beaver Dam Creek","value":"About 14,000 United States troops and roughly 10,000–11,000 Confederates engaged","note":"The episode uses 14,000 and 10,000. The National Park Service states that five A. P. Hill brigades, perhaps 11,000 men, pushed toward the creek. These are approximate battlefield strengths."},{"label":"Jackson at Hundley’s Corner","value":"Reached the Federal flank area late and stopped for the night","note":"The episode places his arrival at about 5 p.m. and his bivouac order at 6 p.m. The National Park Service describes arrival at the creek headwaters late on June 26. Precise timing differs among accounts."},{"label":"Main Confederate engagement","value":"A. P. Hill’s brigades fixed McCall’s division along the creek","note":"Field and Archer engaged from the west bank. Anderson briefly crossed in the upper sector. Pender and later Ripley attacked near Ellerson’s Mill."},{"label":"Ellerson’s Mill attacks","value":"Pender’s and Ripley’s attacks failed under concentrated infantry and artillery fire","note":"The movement occurred over difficult, exposed ground against Truman Seymour’s brigade. The schematic map does not reconstruct exact regimental routes or formations."},{"label":"Ripley’s brigade losses","value":"Nearly 600 killed and wounded","note":"The National Park Service states that Ripley’s brigade incurred almost half of Lee’s losses in the battle."},{"label":"44th Georgia losses","value":"335 killed and wounded from 514 reported engaged","note":"The episode gives these figures and identifies the regiment’s first battle as the attack at Ellerson’s Mill. Contemporary reporting varies in how detached men and later deaths were counted."},{"label":"1st North Carolina losses","value":"About 142 casualties in the episode’s account","note":"Published figures vary slightly by source and category. The regiment also lost much of its field and company leadership."},{"label":"Beaver Dam Creek casualties","value":"Approximately 1,400 Confederates and 350 United States troops","note":"The National Park Service uses these rounded totals. The episode gives about 1,500 Confederate casualties and 361 United States casualties; estimates vary by source and categorization."},{"label":"Tactical result","value":"United States victory at Beaver Dam Creek","note":"No Confederate force broke McCall’s east-bank line on June 26, and the direct attacks failed at heavy cost."},{"label":"Night withdrawal","value":"Porter moved east before dawn on June 27","note":"Concern that Jackson could turn the northern flank led McClellan to order the position abandoned despite the tactical victory."},{"label":"Operational consequence","value":"McClellan yielded the initiative after winning the June 26 battlefield fight","note":"The withdrawal carried Porter toward the Gaines’s Mill position and began the Army of the Potomac’s movement away from the Richmond front."},{"label":"Command failure","value":"The Confederate plan operated without timely coordination among separated columns","note":"Jackson, Branch, A. P. Hill, and Lee lacked an effective flow of current information. The episode assigns ultimate responsibility for army-level staff coordination to Lee while distinguishing that judgment from the observed movements."},{"label":"Recommended book","value":"“Davis and Lee at War” by Steven E. Woodworth","note":"The podcast page and episode closing identify this title as the recommendation."},{"label":"Transcript normalizations","value":"Heintzelman, Kearny, Huger, Beaver Dam Creek, Hundley’s Corner, abatis, and Ellerson’s Mill","note":"The automated transcript produces forms including “Heinsleman,” “Carney,” “Nugez,” “UJ,” “Bosen’s Creek,” and “Ellersons.”"}],"maps":[{"title":"The first two Seven Days battlefields","caption":"Oak Grove lay south of the Chickahominy on the Williamsburg Road, while Mechanicsville and Beaver Dam Creek lay north of the river. The diagram shows the broad operational relationship: McClellan’s June 25 advance, Lee’s concentration against Porter on June 26, and the smaller Confederate force holding the Richmond approaches. Locations, routes, force areas, and distances are generalized. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"National Park Service, U.S. Army Center of Military History, Historic American Landscapes Survey, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep158-seven-days-overview.svg","alt":"Schematic overview of Richmond, the Chickahominy River, Oak Grove, Mechanicsville, Beaver Dam Creek, and the broad locations of the opposing forces."},{"title":"Movements at Mechanicsville and Beaver Dam Creek","caption":"At about 3 p.m., A. P. Hill crossed Meadow Bridge and pushed through Mechanicsville as Porter’s outposts retired to McCall’s prepared line. Hill’s brigades engaged along Beaver Dam Creek; Pender and Ripley attacked near Ellerson’s Mill. Jackson reached the upper creek area late and did not turn the position that day. Arrows show generalized relationships, not exact routes, formations, frontage, or troop geometry. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"National Park Service, Historic American Landscapes Survey, U.S. Army Center of Military History, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep158-mechanicsville-movements.svg","alt":"Schematic troop-movement map of A. P. Hill crossing at Meadow Bridge, the Federal withdrawal to Beaver Dam Creek, Jackson near Hundley’s Corner, and attacks near Ellerson’s Mill."},{"title":"Contemporary view of Ellerson’s Mill","caption":"Alfred R. Waud drew this scene at Ellerson’s Mill on June 26, 1862. It is a participant-era visual record, not a surveyed plan of the troop positions.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep158-ellisons-mill-waud.jpg","alt":"Alfred Waud pencil drawing titled A glimpse of the defeat at Ellisons Mill, dated June 26, 1862."}],"moments":[{"time":"4:51","audioTime":291,"title":"The Seven Days opening is introduced","detail":"The episode places Oak Grove on June 25 before Lee’s planned June 26 offensive."},{"time":"6:03","audioTime":363,"title":"McClellan selects the Oak Grove objective","detail":"The intended advance would move the line toward high ground near Old Tavern for siege artillery."},{"time":"6:21","audioTime":381,"title":"The attacking formations are identified","detail":"Hooker’s and Kearny’s divisions of Heintzelman’s III Corps provide the assaulting brigades."},{"time":"6:52","audioTime":412,"title":"Oak Grove begins","detail":"At about 8:30 a.m., Sickles, Grover, and Robinson advance across the Williamsburg Road sector."},{"time":"7:13","audioTime":433,"title":"Terrain breaks the alignment","detail":"Sickles’s brigade falls behind in thick, swampy woods, leaving Grover unsupported on the right."},{"time":"7:34","audioTime":454,"title":"Confederates counterattack","detail":"Wright resists Grover, and Ransom’s arriving brigade counterattacks toward Sickles."},{"time":"7:54","audioTime":474,"title":"McClellan orders a suspension","detail":"Directing the fight by telegraph from the rear, McClellan tells Heintzelman to withdraw."},{"time":"8:16","audioTime":496,"title":"The advance resumes","detail":"After reaching the field at about 1 p.m., McClellan orders the yielded ground retaken."},{"time":"8:44","audioTime":524,"title":"Oak Grove ends with a limited gain","detail":"Darkness ends the fighting after an advance of about 600 yards."},{"time":"9:10","audioTime":550,"title":"Lee retains his offensive schedule","detail":"The June 25 action does not derail the Confederate movement north of the Chickahominy."},{"time":"10:07","audioTime":607,"title":"McClellan receives warning about Jackson","detail":"Reports indicate that Jackson is moving toward Richmond and may attack the Federal right."},{"time":"12:03","audioTime":723,"title":"Jackson’s planned route is reviewed","detail":"Lee expected a 3 a.m. departure from Slash Church and an early arrival on Porter’s flank."},{"time":"13:29","audioTime":809,"title":"The march starts late","detail":"Jackson’s column begins moving near 5 a.m., already placing Lee’s sequence behind schedule."},{"time":"14:01","audioTime":841,"title":"Obstacles slow Jackson","detail":"United States cavalry fells trees across the roads while the column advances cautiously through unfamiliar terrain."},{"time":"15:22","audioTime":922,"title":"Jackson will not arrive on time","detail":"The episode contrasts Lee’s expected morning arrival with Jackson’s late-afternoon arrival near the Federal flank."},{"time":"16:04","audioTime":964,"title":"A. P. Hill waits at Meadow Bridge","detail":"Hill’s Light Division is in position, but the expected confirmation through Branch does not reach him."},{"time":"20:47","audioTime":1247,"title":"Hill starts the battle","detail":"At about 3 p.m., Hill crosses Meadow Bridge without new authorization or confirmation that Jackson is in place."},{"time":"21:05","audioTime":1265,"title":"Federal outposts leave Mechanicsville","detail":"Porter’s forward troops retire to the prepared line at Beaver Dam Creek."},{"time":"21:42","audioTime":1302,"title":"Lee releases the supporting columns","detail":"Seeing Hill’s movement, Lee orders Longstreet to cross; D. H. Hill’s division leads at the Mechanicsville bridge."},{"time":"24:33","audioTime":1473,"title":"Hill reaches Beaver Dam Creek","detail":"Confederate infantry and artillery enter exposed ground opposite McCall’s prepared position."},{"time":"25:55","audioTime":1555,"title":"Jackson reaches Hundley’s Corner late","detail":"Jackson hears the battle to the south but does not establish contact with Hill or Lee."},{"time":"27:01","audioTime":1621,"title":"Jackson halts for the night","detail":"At about 6 p.m. in the episode’s account, his column goes into bivouac instead of turning the Federal line."},{"time":"27:43","audioTime":1663,"title":"A. P. Hill commits brigades along the creek","detail":"Field and Archer move forward to engage while waiting for the expected flank movement."},{"time":"28:31","audioTime":1711,"title":"The Federal position is described","detail":"The creek, swampy ground, abatis, rifle pits, clear fire lanes, and artillery combine into a strong defense."},{"time":"29:19","audioTime":1759,"title":"Anderson probes the upper sector","detail":"Anderson’s brigade briefly reaches the east bank but cannot break the defended line."},{"time":"30:23","audioTime":1823,"title":"Pender moves toward Ellerson’s Mill","detail":"Poor maps and artillery fire disrupt the movement before two regiments attack Seymour’s position."},{"time":"31:16","audioTime":1876,"title":"Pender’s attack fails","detail":"The attackers approach within about 100 yards before concentrated fire forces them back."},{"time":"32:00","audioTime":1920,"title":"Ripley receives conflicting direction","detail":"Lee, A. P. Hill, D. H. Hill, and Jefferson Davis each influence the brigade’s movement without a unified command channel."},{"time":"33:30","audioTime":2010,"title":"Ripley attacks at Ellerson’s Mill","detail":"The 1st North Carolina and 44th Georgia advance into concentrated infantry and artillery fire."},{"time":"34:02","audioTime":2042,"title":"Robert A. Smith is mortally wounded","detail":"The 44th Georgia’s colonel is struck while leading the regiment’s first battle."},{"time":"34:56","audioTime":2096,"title":"Regimental losses are counted","detail":"The episode gives 142 casualties for the 1st North Carolina and 335 killed and wounded for the 44th Georgia."},{"time":"35:24","audioTime":2124,"title":"The battle totals are summarized","detail":"The episode uses about 1,500 Confederate casualties and 361 United States casualties; official summaries differ slightly."},{"time":"36:08","audioTime":2168,"title":"Lee faults Jackson’s absence","detail":"The episode cites Lee’s postwar statement that he was disappointed not to find Jackson present as expected."},{"time":"37:35","audioTime":2255,"title":"Army-level coordination is assessed","detail":"The episode identifies Lee’s inadequate staff information system as a central cause of the failed execution."},{"time":"39:09","audioTime":2349,"title":"McClellan claims victory","detail":"At 9 p.m., he reports a complete victory to Secretary of War Edwin Stanton."},{"time":"39:41","audioTime":2381,"title":"Porter is ordered to withdraw","detail":"McClellan concludes that Jackson threatens the right and directs a night movement eastward."},{"time":"40:12","audioTime":2412,"title":"The tactical victory yields no held field","detail":"By first light on June 27, only a rear guard remains at Beaver Dam Creek while Porter moves toward Gaines’s Mill."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan","command":"Army of the Potomac","subordinates":"Directed the Oak Grove advance, visited both battlefields, and ordered Porter’s withdrawal after the June 26 victory"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Samuel P. Heintzelman","command":"III Corps at Oak Grove","subordinates":"Divisions of Brig. Gens. Joseph Hooker and Philip Kearny"},{"commander":"Brig. Gens. Daniel E. Sickles, Cuvier Grover, and John C. Robinson","command":"Attacking brigades at Oak Grove","subordinates":"Advanced on the right, center, and left, respectively"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Fitz John Porter","command":"V Corps north of the Chickahominy","subordinates":"Ordered the Mechanicsville outposts back to Beaver Dam Creek and supervised the defense"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. George A. McCall","command":"Pennsylvania Reserves division","subordinates":"Brigades of John F. Reynolds and Truman Seymour held the creek; supporting troops extended and reinforced the line"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#9a3d32","branches":[{"commander":"Gen. Robert E. Lee","command":"Army of Northern Virginia","subordinates":"Committed the June 26 offensive and directed supporting divisions across the Chickahominy"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Benjamin Huger","command":"Division opposing Oak Grove","subordinates":"Brigades including Ambrose R. Wright and Robert Ransom fought the United States advance"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. A. P. Hill","command":"Light Division","subordinates":"Crossed Meadow Bridge and committed brigades under Joseph R. Anderson, James J. Archer, Charles W. Field, and William D. Pender along Beaver Dam Creek"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","command":"Turning column north of the battlefield","subordinates":"Reached the upper Beaver Dam Creek area late and halted without turning McCall’s position that day"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. D. H. Hill","command":"Supporting division","subordinates":"Roswell S. Ripley’s brigade crossed at Mechanicsville and attacked near Ellerson’s Mill"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. James Longstreet","command":"Supporting division","subordinates":"Crossed after A. P. Hill opened the way through Mechanicsville"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Lawrence O. Branch","command":"Link between Jackson and A. P. Hill","subordinates":"Received notice that Jackson was late; whether it reached Hill is unclear in the episode’s account"}]}],"people":[{"name":"A. P. Hill","role":"Light Division commander who initiated the June 26 crossing at Meadow Bridge","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/ep158-ap-hill.jpg"},{"name":"Fitz John Porter","role":"V Corps commander who concentrated the defense at Beaver Dam Creek","side":"United States","image":"/images/ep158-fitz-john-porter.jpg"},{"name":"George B. McClellan","role":"Army of the Potomac commander who ordered the Oak Grove advance and later abandoned the Beaver Dam Creek position","side":"United States"},{"name":"Robert E. Lee","role":"Army of Northern Virginia commander directing his first offensive in army command","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","role":"Turning-column commander whose delayed movement disrupted Lee’s planned sequence","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"George A. McCall","role":"Pennsylvania Reserves division commander at Beaver Dam Creek","side":"United States"},{"name":"Truman Seymour","role":"Brigade commander defending the lower creek sector near Ellerson’s Mill","side":"United States"},{"name":"Joseph Hooker","role":"Division commander whose brigades formed the right and center of the Oak Grove attack","side":"United States"},{"name":"Philip Kearny","role":"Division commander whose Robinson brigade formed the left of the Oak Grove attack","side":"United States"},{"name":"Ambrose R. Wright","role":"Confederate brigade commander who resisted Grover at Oak Grove","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"William D. Pender","role":"Confederate brigade commander whose attack near Ellerson’s Mill failed under concentrated fire","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Roswell S. Ripley","role":"Confederate brigade commander whose regiments suffered severe losses near Ellerson’s Mill","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Jefferson Davis","role":"Confederate president who observed the battle and intervened in direction reaching Ripley","side":"Confederacy"}],"losses":[{"name":"Col. Robert A. Smith","side":"Confederacy","role":"Commander, 44th Georgia Infantry","outcome":"Mortally wounded while leading the regiment’s attack at Ellerson’s Mill. Contemporary reporting recorded 335 killed and wounded in the regiment; counts vary slightly on the number actually in the attacking line."},{"name":"Col. Montford S. Stokes","side":"Confederacy","role":"Commander, 1st North Carolina Infantry","outcome":"Mortally wounded in the attack near Ellerson’s Mill and died in July 1862. Later sources disagree on the exact date of death."}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/ep158-seven-days-overview.svg","alt":"Schematic overview of the first two Seven Days battlefields near Richmond.","caption":"Oak Grove and Beaver Dam Creek occurred on opposite sides of the Chickahominy. The map shows their operational relationship without claiming exact unit positions.","source":"National Park Service, U.S. Army Center of Military History, Historic American Landscapes Survey, and episode transcript; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep158-mechanicsville-movements.svg","alt":"Schematic map of generalized movements at Mechanicsville and Beaver Dam Creek.","caption":"Hill crossed at Meadow Bridge, Federal outposts retired to the creek, and Confederate brigades attacked the prepared line while Jackson remained out of effective contact to the north.","source":"National Park Service, Historic American Landscapes Survey, U.S. Army Center of Military History, and episode transcript; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep158-ellisons-mill-waud.jpg","alt":"Alfred Waud’s June 26, 1862 pencil drawing of the fighting at Ellerson’s Mill.","caption":"Alfred R. Waud made this drawing, titled “A glimpse of the defeat at Ellisons Mill,” on June 26, 1862. It depicts United States defenders and the fortified landscape.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep158-mechanicsville-1865.jpg","alt":"John Reekie photograph of buildings in Mechanicsville, Virginia, in April 1865.","caption":"John Reekie photographed Mechanicsville in April 1865. The image documents the village several years after the June 1862 battle and does not show the battle in progress.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep158-ap-hill.jpg","alt":"Civil War-era photographic portrait of Confederate general A. P. Hill in uniform.","caption":"A. P. Hill crossed Meadow Bridge at about 3 p.m. and began the fighting before receiving confirmation that Jackson had reached the Federal flank.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep158-fitz-john-porter.jpg","alt":"Civil War-era photographic portrait of United States general Fitz John Porter.","caption":"Porter concentrated the V Corps defense behind Beaver Dam Creek and withdrew only after McClellan ordered a night movement east.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."}],"sources":[{"label":"Official Civil War Podcast episode 158 page","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2016/07/17/civil-war-podcast-episode-158/"},{"label":"National Park Service battle summary: Oak Grove","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=va015"},{"label":"Richmond National Battlefield Park: Battle of Beaver Dam Creek","url":"https://www.nps.gov/rich/learn/historyculture/bdcbulletin.htm"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: The Virginia Campaigns, March–August 1862","url":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/75-5.pdf"},{"label":"Library of Congress Historic American Landscapes Survey: Battle of Beaver Dam Creek","url":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/va2109/"},{"label":"Primary visual source: Alfred R. Waud’s Ellerson’s Mill drawing, June 26, 1862","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2004660959/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Mechanicsville photograph by John Reekie, April 1865","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2007685830/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: A. P. Hill portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2022630549/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Fitz John Porter portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018667594/"},{"label":"Official podcast book-recommendations list","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/book-recommendations/"}]}}]}]
18:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section maps-section","id":"maps","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Maps"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"maps-grid","children":[["$","figure","Contemporary view of Ellerson’s Mill",{"className":"reference-map","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"map-frame static-map-frame","children":["$","a",null,{"className":"map-image-link","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep158-ellisons-mill-waud.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","aria-label":"Open Contemporary view of Ellerson’s Mill at full size","children":["$","img",null,{"src":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep158-ellisons-mill-waud.jpg","alt":"Alfred Waud pencil drawing titled A glimpse of the defeat at Ellisons Mill, dated June 26, 1862.","width":1600,"height":1200,"loading":"lazy"}]}]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Contemporary view of Ellerson’s Mill"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Alfred R. Waud drew this scene at Ellerson’s Mill on June 26, 1862. It is a participant-era visual record, not a surveyed plan of the troop positions."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"map-actions","children":[["$","a",null,{"href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep158-ellisons-mill-waud.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","children":["Open full-size map ",["$","$Le",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":"$d:props:children:1:props:children:0:props:children:1:props:children:2:props:iconNode","className":"lucide-arrow-up-right","size":13}]]}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."}]]}],"$undefined"]}]]}]]}]]}]
19:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section","id":"people","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"People and command"}],[[["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css"}],["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css"}],["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css"}],"$undefined"],[["$","div",null,{"className":"command-grid reference-command","children":[["$","section","United States",{"className":"command-column #315f8c","children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"United States"}],[["$","div","Army of the Potomac-Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Army of the Potomac"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Directed the Oak Grove advance, visited both battlefields, and ordered Porter’s withdrawal after the June 26 victory"}]]}],["$","div","III Corps at Oak Grove-Maj. Gen. Samuel P. Heintzelman",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"III Corps at Oak Grove"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Maj. Gen. Samuel P. Heintzelman"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"